Since the upgrade to Gradle 9 (OFBIZ-13208), several tasks in {{build.gradle}} trigger "Invocation of Task.project at execution time has been deprecated. This will fail with an error in Gradle 10". Their {{doLast}}/{{doFirst}} actions read {{project.hasProperty}}/{{project.property}}, {{rootProject}}, {{sourceSets}} and {{file()}} from the project while the task runs, for example in {{generateEncryptedSecret}}:

{code:groovy}
doLast {
    def secret = project.hasProperty('secretPassword') ? project.property('secretPassword')
            : promptSecret('Enter secret value: ')
{code}

The affected tasks include {{generateDBPassword}}, {{generateEncryptedSecret}}, {{reEncryptAllSecrets}}, {{gitInfoFooter}}, {{loadTenant}}, {{generateDatabaseTemplateFile}}, {{createPlugin}}, {{installPlugin}}, {{uninstallPlugin}}, {{pullPlugin}} and {{deletePluginDocumentation}}. Resolve these values when the task is configured, as providers ({{providers.gradleProperty}}, {{providers.environmentVariable}}, a local reference to {{sourceSets.main.runtimeClasspath}}, {{layout.projectDirectory}}), and only read those providers inside the task actions.

h3. Acceptance criteria
* Running each of the tasks above with {{--warning-mode all}} shows no "Invocation of Task.project at execution time" warning.
* The tasks behave as before, including the interactive prompts when a -P property is omitted.

We hit a login loop on 24.09 with security.login.http.header set. The user logs in with username and password (request without the header), the login itself succeeds and redirects to main, but the next request is answered with 401 and the login page again.

This only happens for users whose UserLogin.hasLoggedOut is null in the database, which is the case for every user that never logged out and never logged in through the header (users created by createUserLogin, the admin loaded by loadAdminUserLogin, etc.).

It looks like a regression of OFBIZ-10506. That fix (266b63ac12) relies on getBoolean returning null for a missing value:

{code:java}
Boolean hasLoggedOut = userLogin.getBoolean("hasLoggedOut");
...
if (hasLoggedOut == null || hasLoggedOut) {
    userLogin.set("hasLoggedOut", "N");
}
...
} else if (hasLoggedOut != null && !hasLoggedOut) {
    doStore = false;
}
{code}

Since OFBIZ-12386 (36b82f2b16) GenericEntity.getBoolean returns false instead of null, so for a null flag the "N" is never set and doStore becomes false. The userLogin put in the session keeps hasLoggedOut = null and the database row is not updated.

With header login enabled, checkRequestHeaderLogin calls LoginWorker.isUserLoggedIn on every request, and isUserLoggedIn only accepts "N", so it removes userLogin from the session and checkLogin returns 401. Without header login nothing calls isUserLoggedIn, which is probably why it went unnoticed. The same happens with USERNAME/PASSWORD

When OFBiz is started with a port offset (--portoffset=N), WebSiteProperties.addPortOffset() calls Integer.valueOf() on the https port, which is blank by default (port.https= in url.properties, and the demo WebStore WebSite has no httpsPort). The resulting NumberFormatException breaks every link built through OfbizUrlBuilder, so the ecommerce pages (header, footer, search results...) fail to render. Without a port offset the blank port is never parsed and the problem does not show.

h3. Steps to reproduce
# {{./gradlew cleanAll loadAll "ofbiz --start --portoffset=30"}}
# Open {{https://localhost:8473/ecomseo}}
# The page fails with {{java.lang.NumberFormatException: For input string: ""}} from {{WebSiteProperties.addPortOffset}}

A blank port should be left blank when the offset is applied, and the localhost fallback to 8443 in OfbizUrlBuilder should also take the offset into account.

h3. Summary

{{ExternalLoginKeysManager}} mints a single {{externalLoginKey}} per screen render and shares it across every cross-webapp link the render emits (see the hardening in [PR #1710|https://github.com/apache/ofbiz-framework/pull/1710], which made a key redeemable once per destination webapp rather than once globally). Each {{ExternalLoginTicket}} carries a {{targetContextPath}} field intended to scope a key to its intended destination webapp, but the only place a ticket is constructed, {{ExternalLoginKeysManager.getExternalLoginKey()}}, always passes {{null}} for it:

{code:java}
EXTERNAL_LOGIN_KEYS.put(externalKey, new ExternalLoginTicket(userLogin, null));
{code}

{{ExternalLoginTicket#isValidFor}} treats a {{null}} {{targetContextPath}} as "valid for any webapp":

{code:java}
boolean isValidFor(HttpServletRequest request) {
    if (System.currentTimeMillis() > expiresAtMillis) {
        return false;
    }
    return targetContextPath == null || targetContextPath.equals(request.getServletContext().getContextPath());
}
{code}

As a result, a captured {{externalLoginKey}} (address bar, browser history, Referer header, proxy log) is redeemable once against *every* webapp reachable on the same OFBiz server within the 2-minute TTL, not just the webapps the page that minted it actually linked to. This includes webapps such as {{webtools}} that are deliberately excluded from the app-switcher navigation shown to a given user.

