{SecuredUpload.isValidFile}} applies the SVG content check ({{isValidSvgFile}}) only for the "ImageAndSvg" file type. For "All" and "AllButCompressed", {{isValidTextFile}} is evaluated first and accepts SVG files, so the SVG check is not reached. The same applies to SVG files inside a zip ("Compressed", whose entries are checked as "AllButCompressed") and to SVG content in a .txt file ("Text").

{code:java}
default: // All
    if (isValidTextFile(fileToCheck, true)
            || isValidImageIncludingSvgFile(fileToCheck)
            ...
{code}

h3. Proposed fix
In {{isValidFile}}, before the file-type checks, require any file with an SVG extension or SVG content to pass {{isValidSvgFile}}, whatever the file type.

Two related defects in the XSLT DataResource rendering path.

*1. XInclude is left enabled on the XSLT data parser (framework/base XslTransform.java)*

{{XslTransform.renderTemplate()}} parses the XML data document with a SAX parser configured {{setXIncludeAware(true)}}. The external-entity hardening added alongside it (OFBIZ commit "Enhance XML processing security by disabling external entity resolution") does not cover XInclude, which is a separate resolution mechanism. A data document can therefore pull in arbitrary local files via {{<xi:include href="file://..."/>}} (local file disclosure, bypassing the LOCAL_FILE/OFBIZ_FILE allow-list that only gates the rendered file itself) or fetch arbitrary URLs via an {{http}} include (SSRF). Every other hardened XML parser in the framework disables external resolution; this parser should be consistent.

Fix: set {{setXIncludeAware(false)}}, matching the rest of the framework.

*2. XSLT branch crashes when the active theme has no VT_DOCBOOKSTYLESHEET (content DataResourceWorker.java)*

The XSLT branch reads:

{code:java}
String docbookStylesheet = modelTheme.getProperty("VT_DOCBOOKSTYLESHEET").toString();
File sourceFileLocation = new File(System.getProperty("ofbiz.home") + "/themes"
        + docbookStylesheet.substring(1, docbookStylesheet.length() - 1));
{code}

{{ModelTheme.getProperty()}} returns an empty string when the property is absent, and no shipped visual theme defines {{VT_DOCBOOKSTYLESHEET}}, so {{"".

checkShoppingListSecurity and checkShoppingListItemSecurity (ShoppingListServicesScript.groovy) build the permission as {{"_${parameters.permissionAction}"}}, but they implement {{permissionInterface}}, which passes the action as {{mainAction}} and the calling service as {{resourceDescription}}. {{permissionAction}} is never set, so the check is {{PARTYMGR_null}} and only PARTYMGR_ADMIN passes. Users with PARTYMGR_UPDATE or PARTYMGR_DELETE are wrongly denied on another party's shopping list, and the OrderSecurityErrorToRunForAnotherParty message shows empty or literal ${parentMethodName}/${permissionAction} placeholders. This dates from OFBIZ-9763, which moved these checks from simple-methods into permission services.

{code:java}
security.hasEntityPermission('PARTYMGR', "_${parameters.permissionAction}", parameters.userLogin)
{code}

calculateShoppingListDeepTotalPrice also calls checkShoppingListItemSecurity without any action, while the original minilang used VIEW.

Acceptance criteria: the checks use {{mainAction}}, the error message is filled from {{resourceDescription}}/{{mainAction}}, and calculateShoppingListDeepTotalPrice checks with the VIEW action.

In {{PdfSurveyServices.getInputByteBuffer}}, the {{contentId}} branch does not check the result of the Content lookup:

{code:java}
GenericValue content = EntityQuery.use(delegator).from("Content").where("contentId", contentId).cache().queryOne();
String dataResourceId = content.getString("dataResourceId");
{code}

When the {{contentId}} matches no Content record, {{content}} is null and the survey PDF services ({{buildSurveyFromPdf}}, {{buildSurveyResponseFromPdf}}, {{getAcroFieldsFromPdf}}, {{setAcroFieldsFromSurveyResponse}}) fail with a NullPointerException ("Service invocation error") instead of a clear error. A Content without a DataResource also fails, with a confusing "Cannot stream null data RESOURCE!" message.

Both cases need to be treated like a missing PDF input, returning the existing {{ContentPDFInputMissing}} service error added in OFBIZ-13688.

h3. Problem
Since OFBIZ-13698, {{DocumentIndexer}} leaves the index unchanged when {{prepareDocument()}} throws. Two places still catch and only log database errors, so the document is indexed with fields missing:
* {{ContentDocument}} calls {{ContentWorker.getContentAncestryAll()}}, which catches {{GenericEntityException}}: the content is indexed without its {{site}} field.
* {{ProductDocument}} catches {{IOException | GeneralException}} around {{DataResourceWorker.renderDataResourceAsText()}}: the product is indexed without that content text.

{code:java}
} catch (IOException | GeneralException e1) {
    Debug.logError(e1, "Error getting content text to index", MODULE);
}
{code}

h3. Fix
{{ContentDocument}} needs an ancestry lookup that throws on database errors, and {{ProductDocument}} needs to rethrow {{GenericEntityException}}, while still logging other rendering errors so that a broken content does not block the re-indexing of the product. Applies to trunk and release24.09.

h3. Problem
{{ProductDocument.prepareDocument()}} and {{ContentDocument.prepareDocument()}} log and swallow entity and rendering exceptions and return {{null}}, and {{DocumentIndexer.run()}} treats a {{null}} document as a removed record and calls {{indexWriter.deleteDocuments()}}. Any transient database error while indexing therefore silently removes the product or content from the Lucene index.

{code:java}
} catch (GenericEntityException e) {
    Debug.logError(e, MODULE);
}
return null; // DocumentIndexer then deletes the document from the index
{code}

h3. Fix
{{LuceneDocument.prepareDocument()}} needs to throw when the document cannot be prepared, keeping {{null}} for "remove from the index", and {{DocumentIndexer}} needs to log and skip such a document, leaving the index unchanged. Applies to trunk and release24.09.

Several places convert a resolved location URL to a file path with {{URL.getFile()}} / {{URL.getPath()}}, which keep the percent-encoding ({{%20}}). When the OFBiz home directory contains a space, the resulting {{File}} does not exist. The most visible case is {{ApacheFopWorker.getFactoryInstance()}}: {{fop.xconf}} is reported as not found, {{fopFactory}} stays null and every PDF render fails with a NullPointerException (e.g. {{testFopMacroLibrary}}, {{OrderNotificationTests.testSendOrderConfirmation}}).

{code:java}
URL configFilePath = FlexibleLocation.resolveLocation(FOP_PATH + "/fop.xconf");
File userConfigFile = FileUtil.getFile(configFilePath.getFile());
{code}

The same pattern exists in {{ScriptUtil.executeScript}}, {{CommonEvents.openSourceFile}}, {{MiniLangUtil.writeMiniLangDocument}}, {{ScaleImage}}, {{ImageManagementServices}} and {{UtilURL.getOfbizHomeRelativeLocation}}. These should convert via {{URL.toURI()}} (or read via {{URL.openStream()}}) instead.

Acceptance criteria: widget and order integration tests pass when OFBiz is run from a directory whose path contains a space.

When logged in using the admin user on the Ecommerce homepage, the viewprofile screen is showing a FTL error:
{code:java}
An error has occurred when reading existing sub-variable "maritalStatusEnumId"; see cause exception! The type of the containing value was: extended_hash+method+string (org.apache.ofbiz.entity.GenericValue wrapped into f.e.b.MapModel) ---- FTL stack trace ("~" means nesting-related): - Failed at: #if person.maritalStatusEnumId?has_co... [in template "component://ecommerce/template/customer/ViewProfile.ftl" at line 92, column 11] ---- {code}

The mini-lang {{<property-to-field>}} resolved properties through {{EntityUtilProperties}}, which checks the {{SystemProperty}} table before the {{.properties}} file. The converted scripts call {{UtilProperties}} directly, so a SystemProperty value is silently ignored. Resolve by reading via {{EntityUtilProperties.getPropertyValue(resource, name, default, delegator)}}, converting types by hand (the typed {{EntityUtilProperties.getPropertyAs*}} variants take no delegator and would not help).

*Properties with seed rows loaded by default*
* {{DataServicesScript}}: {{content.upload.always.local.file}}
* {{ImageManagementServicesScript}}: {{image.management.autoApproveImage}}, {{image.management.multipleApproval}}

*Other affected reads*
* {{EmailServicesScript}}: {{general#defaultFromEmailAddress}}
* {{CostServices}}: {{arithmetic#finaccount.decimals}}, {{finaccount.roundingGroovyMethod}}
* {{PaymentServices}}, {{RateServices}}, {{InventoryServicesScript}}: {{general#currency.uom.id.default}}
* {{OrderServicesScript}} (trunk only): {{shipment.default.cost_actual_over_estimated_percent_allowed}}

h3. 1. {{quickReturnFromOrder()}} creates a spurious null-amount adjustment on every call
{code:java}
Map serviceResultART = run service: 'getOrderAvailableReturnedTotal', with: orderAvailableCtx
BigDecimal availableReturnTotal = serviceResult.availableReturnTotal
{code}
Reads the stale {{serviceResult}} variable (from an earlier, unrelated {{createReturnHeader}} call) instead of {{serviceResultART}}. {{createReturnHeader}}'s result has no {{availableReturnTotal}} field, so this is always null, and {{if (availableReturnTotal != BigDecimal.ZERO)}} is therefore always true — every call unconditionally creates a balance adjustment with {{amount: null}}, even for a perfectly balanced return.

